---
import { Card, CardGrid } from "@astrojs/starlight/components";
import Demo from "../components/Demo.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Collapsible from "../components/Collapsible.astro";

import PrivEval from "../assets/demos/PrivEval.png";
import ClustersInFocusImage from "../assets/demos/clusters-in-focus.png";
import NeurodegenVisImage from "../assets/demos/neurodegen-vis.png";
import OnSetImage from "../assets/demos/onset.png";
import GIBBERImage from "../assets/demos/gibber.png";
import ParamInterImage from "../assets/demos/param-inter.png";
import GutBrainImage from "../assets/demos/gut-brain-viewer.png";
import InSilicoImage from "../assets/demos/in-silico-cancer-cell-simulation.png";
import NeuronalImage from "../assets/demos/neuronal-brain-simulation.png";
import AlvisImage from "../assets/demos/alvis.png";
import DiffCellSigImage from "../assets/demos/diff-cell-sig.png";
import TTYGImage from "../assets/demos/talk-to-your-graph.png";
import EEGViewer from "../assets/demos/niklas-eeg-viewer.png";
import XEEGImage from "../assets/demos/xeeg.png";
import FederatedImage from "../assets/demos/federated.png";
import HereditaryFLImage from "../assets/demos/hereditary-fl.png";
import StarlightPage from "@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro";
import { getImage } from "astro:assets";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
const systems = await getCollection("systems");
console.log("systems", systems);
---

<StarlightPage frontmatter={{ title: "Test", template: "splash" }}>
  Within the Horizon Europe Project [HEREDITARY](https://hereditary-project.eu/)
  (running from 2024-2027), we collaborate with different partners from medical
  research, machine learning, databases and other fields on the visualization of
  multimodal, distributed clinical and research data, building various
  visualisation prototypes and demos. Many of the demos are developed as part of
  the Work Package 5 (WP5) efforts, which focusses on visualisation. This
  webpage demonstrates many of the visualization approaches we are developing,
  making them available for interested parties to try out. For any questions,
  comments or suggestions please contact the TU Graz HEREDITARY team at
  demos.hereditary@mlist.tugraz.at.

  <Collapsible title="More Information">
    Within the Horizon Europe project
    [HEREDITARY](https://hereditary-project.eu/) (HetERogeneous sEmantic Data
    integratIon for the guT-bRain interplaY, GA 101137074), TU Graz works in
    close collaboration with several partners across the consortium, including
    experts in medical research, federated infrastructures, machine learning,
    data management and related fields, to develop innovative approaches for the
    visualisation of multimodal, distributed clinical and research data.
    Together, they addresses key challenges in the study of neurodegenerative
    diseases and explores neural interactions along the gut-brain axis. This
    includes the development and evaluation of interactive visualisations that
    support the exploration of diverse medical data types, including images,
    biosignals, time series and high-dimensional observational data. A
    particular focus of this work is the integration of Large Language Model
    technologies, in form of conversational interfaces that help users interact
    with data, understand visual representations and identify meaningful
    patterns through natural language. This webpage showcases a selection of the
    visualisation approaches being developed within the HEREDITARY project,
    making them accessible for exploration by interested users in a wider
    community.

    {/* HetERogeneous sEmantic Data integratIon for the guT-bRain interplaY */}
    {
      /* Specific domain questions address studying neurodegenerative diseases, and explore neural interactions along the gut-brain axis. */
    }
    {
      /* We develop, apply and evaluate interactive visualizations for multimodal medical data, including imaging data, bio-signal data, time series data, and high-dimensional observational data. */
    }
    {
      /* A focus of our work is to include Large Language Model technologies in form of chat agents, which help in answering natural language questions by users regarding input data, visual representations, and explain patterns. */
    }
  </Collapsible>

  <CardGrid stagger>
    {
      systems.map((system) => (
        <Demo
          title={system.data.title}
          image={system.data.image}
          link={system.data.link}
          local_link={system.data.local_link}
          code={system.data.code}
          paper={system.data.paper}
          paper-title={system.data["paper-title"]}
          video={system.data.video}
          contact={system.data.contact}
        >
          {system.data.description}
        </Demo>
      ))
    }
    <Demo
      title="Clusters in Focus"
      image={ClustersInFocusImage}
      link="https://hereditary.cgv.tugraz.at/cif"
      code="https://github.com/hereditary-eu/ClustersInFocus"
      paper="https://doi.org/10.2312/vcbm.20251250"
      paper-title="Clusters in Focus: A Simple and Robust Detail-On-Demand Dashboard for Patient Data"
      video="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=juTT_xgT6Yw"
      contact="Peter Waldert"
    >
      Exploring tabular datasets to understand how different feature pairs
      partition data into meaningful cohorts is crucial in domains such as
      biomarker discovery, yet comparing clusters across multiple feature pair
      projections is challenging. We introduce Clusters in Focus, an interactive
      visual analytics dashboard designed to address this gap.
    </Demo>
    <Demo
      title="Neurodegen-Vis"
      image={NeurodegenVisImage}
      link="https://hereditary.cgv.tugraz.at/neurodegen-vis/"
      code="https://github.com/hereditary-eu/Neurodegen-Vis"
      paper="https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-4172/paper1.pdf"
      paper-title="Privacy-Preserving Visual Exploration of Healthcare Data with a Language Model Integration"
      video="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Dx4C1wNZ3Fk"
      contact="Peter Waldert"
    >
      Healthcare records, measurements and datasets can quickly become highly
      complex in terms of storage, anonymisation and interpretation. With
      Neurodegen-Vis, we present a practical toolkit to anonymise, visualise and
      help interpret medical data in a Visual Analytics dashboard, with seamless
      integration of a Large Language Model (LLM).
    </Demo>
    <Demo
      title="OnSET"
      image={OnSetImage}
      link="https://onset.cgv.tugraz.at/"
      code="https://github.com/hereditary-eu/onset"
      paper="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3726302.3730148"
      paper-title="OnSET: Ontology and Semantic Exploration Toolkit"
      video="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q2ZeioBnfhQ"
      contact="Benedikt Kantz"
    >
      OnSET is designed to enable a more explorative ontology querying
      experience for non-expert users, and to build a quick understanding of the
      scale and nature of a KG that is in use. The toolkit consists of multiple
      smaller systems, complimenting each other.
    </Demo>
    <Demo
      title="GIBBER"
      image={GIBBERImage}
      local_link="./systems/gibber"
      link="https://hereditary.cgv.tugraz.at/gibber/"
      code="https://github.com/hereditary-eu/gibber"
      video="https://cloud.tugraz.at/index.php/s/zzGoge8TcrABtGe"
      contact="Benedikt Kantz"
    >
      GIBBER helps experts to associate Diseases, Genes, and Brain regions!
    </Demo>
    <Demo
      title="ParamInter"
      image={ParamInterImage}
      link="https://hereditary.cgv.tugraz.at/param-inter/"
      code="https://github.com/Dakantz/ParamInter"
      paper="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19202"
      paper-title="AlloyInter: Visualising Alloy Mixture Interpolations in t-SNE Representations"
      contact="Benedikt Kantz"
    >
      ParamInter is a novel toolset to explore continous input-output parameter
      sets interactively through guidance by XAI and UCQ. The approach
      interpolates between selected input samples over an embedded subspace,
      guiding the user towards required solutions for their target output
      parameters.
    </Demo>
    <Demo
      title="Gut-Brain Viewer"
      image={GutBrainImage}
      link="https://hereditary.cgv.tugraz.at/gut-brain-viewer"
      code="https://github.com/hereditary-eu/gut-brain-visualization"
      video="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ImEzEN0JuEc"
      contact="Casper van Leeuwen"
    >
      The Gut-Brain Viewer visualizes gut microbiota-brain links from LICA
      outputs via three linked views. An interactive microbiota matrix shows
      organism Z-scores per component with thresholding and selection. A
      modality stacked-bar view displays percent contributions and loads the
      selected modality. Interactive brain slices and volumetric renders show
      voxel Z-scores for SN, ECN, or DMN from 4D NIfTI files.
    </Demo>
    <Demo
      title="Multi-Component State Model Visualisation"
      image={InSilicoImage}
      link="https://in-silico-cancer-cell.waldert.at/"
      code="https://github.com/MrP01/InSilicoCancerCell/"
      paper="https://conferences.eg.org/vcbm2025/wp-content/uploads/sites/26/2025/09/Optimization-of-a-Parametrized-Time-Dependent-Hidden-Markov-Model-by-Example-of-an-Electrophysiological-Cancer-Cell.pdf"
      paper-title="Optimization of a Parametrized, Time-Dependent Hidden Markov Model by Example of an Electrophysiological Cancer Cell"
      contact="Peter Waldert"
    >
      This component is concerned with the visualisation of a multi-component
      state model coupled (biological) system. Each component can be modelled
      through its own individual state model, including dependencies on the
      environment such as ion concentrations, voltages or other variables.
    </Demo>
    <Demo
      title="EEGlass"
      image={XEEGImage}
      link="https://hereditary.cgv.tugraz.at/all-in-on-eeg/"
      paper-title="EEGlass: A Fully Interpretable EEG Dementia Screening Assistant"
      contact="Peter Waldert"
    >
      To make clinical diagnoses more understandable, we present a visual
      interactive dashboard on top of the efficient xEEG classifier network
      architecture, which puts a special emphasis on explainibility of the model
      and outcome. This dataset and model focusses on Alzheimer's Disease (AD)
      and Frontotemporal Dementia (FTD), every step in the classification is
      presented in a visual and understandable way, on top of an integrated EEG
      database viewer.
    </Demo>
    <Demo
      title="Differential Cell Signaling"
      image={DiffCellSigImage}
      link="https://hereditary.cgv.tugraz.at/differential-cell-signaling/"
      code="https://github.com/hereditary-eu/Differential-Cell-Signaling"
      contact="Gaia Tussardi"
    >
      An interactive dashboard for exploring differential cell-signaling
      networks by integrating cell-cell communication inference with
      transcription factor (TF) activities inferred from scRNA-seq. It
      highlights the ligands, receptors, TFs, and cell types that drive disease
      mechanisms, traces signaling loops, and supports on-the-fly GO, KEGG, and
      Reactome over-representation analyses. An Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis
      (ALS) case study is included, enabling the biomedical inspection of
      sporadic ALS, familial ALS, and their contrasts.
    </Demo>
    <Demo
      title="PrivEval: A Tool For Interactive Evaluation Of Privacy Metrics In Synthetic Data Generation"
      image={PrivEval}
      code="https://github.com/hereditary-eu/PrivEval"
      paper="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.14778/3750601.3750649"
      paper-title="PrivEval: A Tool for Interactive Evaluation of Privacy Metrics in Synthetic Data Generation"
      contact="Frederik Marinus Trudslev"
    >
      Synthetic data generation is the process of generating a new synthetic
      dataset based on the statistical properties of a confidential existing
      dataset. Differential privacy is the property of such mechanism that
      establishes the level of privacy, when sharing such data. To ensure
      differential privacy, noise is injected into the statistics learned from
      the dataset, entailing a trade-off between privacy and utility. Privacy is
      then measured via a set of privacy metrics that usually establish a lower
      bound on a few aspects of the privacy-utility tradeoff. PrivEval is a tool
      to make privacy assessment more transparent through evaluation of several
      privacy metrics on both a single user and the overall dataset.
    </Demo>
    <Demo
      title="Flower Learning Dashboard"
      image={HereditaryFLImage}
      code="https://github.com/kalpathy/hereditary_fl"
      contact="Jayashree Kalpathy-Cramer"
    >
      An interactive Streamlit dashboard for exploring Federated Learning (FL)
      concepts, including domain shift, aggregation strategies, and the benefits
      of FL for biased data distributions. Based on the Flower framework.
    </Demo>
    <Demo
      title="Federated Learning Dashboard"
      image={FederatedImage}
      contact="Peter Waldert"
    >
      Dashboard for tracking and understanding the training of federated machine
      learning models, allowing individual parties to view their local dataset,
      contribution to training and compare local to global data.
    </Demo>
    <Demo title="Alvis" image={AlvisImage} contact="Peter Waldert">
      Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) is a fatal neurodegenerative disease
      affecting over 300,000 people worldwide. Currently, there is no cure—only
      treatments aimed at improving patients' quality of life. We introduce
      ALviS, a prototype designed for analyzing ALS datasets, providing a
      foundational framework for visualizing and exploring the multivariate ALS
      dataset.
    </Demo>
    <Demo
      title="Neuronal Lens"
      image={NeuronalImage}
      link="https://hereditary.cgv.tugraz.at/neuronal-lens/"
      paper-title="Interactive 3D Exploration of Large-Scale Neuronal Network Simulations with a Graph Lens"
      contact="Peter Waldert"
    >
      The Neuronal Lens tool visualises a simulation of the brain, focussing on
      the brain connectome and activity data. On the left, we have a shared view
      between the neuron graph and a perspective view, with an integrated lens.
      On the right, the varying quantities described above are displayed, for
      one selected neuron, as a coloured line chart. The colour intensity is
      used to indicate areas of high activity, although that can be adjusted.
    </Demo>
    <Demo
      title="Talk To Your Gene-Disease Graph"
      image={TTYGImage}
      documentation="https://graphdb.ontotext.com/documentation/11.3/talk-to-graph.html"
      contact="Ivelina Nikolova-Koleva"
    >
      By enabling users to communicate in natural language rather than technical
      query languages like SPARQL, Talk To Your Graph (TTYG) reduces technical
      barriers for non-expert users. TTYG enables intuitive access to complex
      data while remaining independent of the underlying schema and resilient to
      subsequent modifications in the knowledge graph's organization. The Talk
      To Your Gene-Disease Graph demonstrator is able to answer natural language
      questions on a custom-curated knowledge graph about gene-disease
      associations. It allows for identification of novel biological targets of
      new potential drug agents and for understanding of the disease biology -
      related biological processes, mechanism, tissue/organ/cell profiling,
      interacting partners, etc.
    </Demo>
    <Demo
      title="Visual Inspection of EEG Data Frame and Model"
      image={EEGViewer}
      contact="Niklas Tscheppe"
    >
      EEG measurements are an important biosignal used to assess brain health.
      For example, EEG helps diagnose neurodegenerative diseases and monitor
      patient status. Selecting which EEG signals to use for model training is
      time-consuming and often uncertain. Visualization tools can help screen
      EEG time windows and make selection decisions faster. A dashboard should
      display OK/NOK labels and allow comparison against clusters, frequency
      bands, and sensors.
    </Demo>
  </CardGrid>

  <Footer />
</StarlightPage>
